on-arrival: general_strike_fever = 1
view-if: strike_triggered = 1
tags: event

= The General Strike

A General Strike has broken out in the UK, with the besieged Coal Miners leading the effort, but sympathy strikes breaking out in every industry from , Chanting "Not a penny off the pay, not a minute on the day"

[? if labour_in_government == 0: With the strike breaking out while we're in opposition, we are faced with a choice. Do we publically stand with the strike, and potencially boost the morale of the strikers by giving it further legitimacy at the cost of escalating fears around our perceived radicalness and maybe alienating the liberals. Or do we keep our distance. A move most of the party leadership prefer, and just focus on advancing our agenda throught strictly parliamentary means.  ?]

[? if chancellor_party == "KPD" or president == "Thälmann" : The KPD's paramilitary, the RFB, is leading the battle, and we are their co-combatants. ?]

On the striking side, The TUC have [+ rb_strength +] thousand members. [? if dissent > 0.15 and dissent <= 0.25: It is somewhat weakened by divisions between it and the Labour Party, and some members choose not to fight. ?][? if dissent > 0.25 and dissent < 0.5: It is weakened by divisions between it and the Labour Party, and many members choose not to fight. ?][? if dissent >= 0.5: It is heavily weakened by divisions between it and the Labour Party, and a substantial portion of members choose not to fight. ?]

[? if rb_militancy < 0.25 : Unfortunately, the TUC are poorly manned and equipped, and will most likely not be able to hold out for long. ?][? if rb_militancy >= 0.25 and rb_militancy < 0.5 : The TUC are coming into this somewhat prepared, having build up a modest warchest to hold out off of.We'll see how long they can persist. ?][? if rb_militancy >= 0.5 : The TUC have are coming into this as prepared as they could ever be. With a hefty warchest, they could go for several months at least! Let's see how this goes down. ?]

[? if labour == "opposition": Do we get involved? ?]

- @stay_out_of_it: We have to keep out of it
- @side_with_strike: We should stand with the strike!

@stay_out_of_it
subtitle: The left of the party will think us cowards.

We refuse to get involved with the strike.

@side_with_strike
subtitle: This could alienate the liberal's support

We stand with the strike!


[? if labour_in_government == 1: The strike has broken out, how do we proceed ?]

The mass strike occurring under a Labour government, does put us in a pickle. While we do want to maintain our image as a serious non extreme party, going as far as to sending in the military could massively hurt our support. That being said, we could leverage our position to try to get the mine owners to concede, but whether the liberals will go with it. We need to pick carefully.

- @attempt_to_negotiate: Strike a compromise
- @side_with_strikers: Side with strikers
- @send_in_military: Send in Military

@attempt_to_negotiate
title: Strike a compromise
on-arrival: unemployed_labour -= 3; workers_labour -= 4; new_middle_labour -= 5; old_middle_labour -= 5; rural_labour -= 4;

It might be best to try and broker a compromise between the two sides. 

@side_with_strikers
title: Side with strikers

Given our position in power, we could use state to pressure the mine owners to concede to the strikers demands.

@send_in_military
title: Send in the military

While we support the right to strike, this is too much. We must use the state to restore order.

